WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.556 --> 00:00:09.760
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:10.230
[NOISE]

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:15.042
Hello, today you are joining to me on a
field trip to Russia.

00:00:15.042 --> 00:00:18.444
Three centuries ago, Russian czar Peter the
Great

00:00:18.444 --> 00:00:22.120
decided to establish the Russian Academy of
Sciences.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:27.300
The only problem was: there were no
scientists in Russia at that time.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.560
It didn't stop the czar, and he traveled
throughout Europe,

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.880
trying to convince leading European
scientists to relocate to Russia.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.960
He did not succeed, since Russia was
considered an unpredictable and even

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.880
dangerous country at that time.
However, Johan Bernoulli, the leading

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:53.081
European mathematician, passed the invitation
to his student Leonhard Euler

00:00:53.081 --> 00:00:54.377
[INAUDIBLE].

00:00:54.377 --> 00:00:58.314
And in 1727, the 20-years-old Euler
[INAUDIBLE].

00:00:58.314 --> 00:00:59.457
[INAUDIBLE].

00:00:59.457 --> 00:01:00.600
arrived to

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.743
[INAUDIBLE]

00:01:01.743 --> 00:01:06.950
cold Saint Petersburg, and he eventually became
one of

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:12.190
the world's greatest mathematicians.
In 1735,

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:12.645
Euler solved 

00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:14.930
the famous "Seven Bridges of Konigsberg" Problem.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.655
The city of Konigsberg, in the kingdom of

00:01:17.655 --> 00:01:21.275
Prussia, was built on two sides of the
river

00:01:21.275 --> 00:01:22.560
Pregel,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.350
and consisted of two islands that were
connected

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:30.105
by seven bridges with each other and with
the mainland.

00:01:30.105 --> 00:01:35.460
The problem was to find a walk through the
city that would cross

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.170
every bridge exactly once and return back.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.020
Euler not only solved this problem for the small

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.520
city of Konigsberg with just seven
bridges,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.040
he developed a universal idea on how

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.025
to solve such problems for imaginary
cities with millions of islands

00:01:52.025 --> 00:01:53.200
[INAUDIBLE]

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.317
and millions of bridges.

00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:57.150
We are in Konigsberg. 

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.982
This is the one of the bridges, 

00:01:58.982 --> 00:01:59.722
[INAUDIBLE].

00:01:59.722 --> 00:02:03.430
this is one of the islands, 
this is another island, 

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.292
and this is the Pregel river. 

00:02:05.292 --> 00:02:05.879
[INAUDIBLE].

00:02:05.879 --> 00:02:07.065
And our goal today is to find a walk

00:02:07.065 --> 00:02:07.690
[INAUDIBLE]

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:13.350
through the city that would visit every
bridge exactly once.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.200
There are many strategies to explore -

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.670
 - let's go - I'll meet you at the next bridge.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.095
Wait a second - 

00:02:21.095 --> 00:02:27.015
what do seven bridges of Konigsberg 
have to do with bioinformatics?

00:02:27.015 --> 00:02:29.460
It may come as a surprise, but without Euler, 

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.065
[INAUDIBLE],

00:02:30.065 --> 00:02:32.705
we probably wouldn't be able to assemble
genomes today!

00:02:32.705 --> 00:02:38.965
We can think about your genome as a giant 
3 billion nulceotide-long text. 

00:02:38.965 --> 00:02:43.755
Four decades ago,
biologists figure out how to read genomes,

00:02:43.755 --> 00:02:48.070
but, they still don't have technology that
would be able to

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:51.578
read the genome the same way we read a book, 
from the first nucleotide till the last nucleotide.

00:02:51.578 --> 00:02:55.135
What they can do though, is to read

00:02:55.135 --> 00:02:56.962
short snippets of the genome called reads, 

00:02:56.962 --> 00:02:57.919
[INAUDIBLE],

00:02:57.919 --> 00:03:03.150
which may be very short, like 200
or 300 nucleotides.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:08.330
The problem, however, is that biologists
do not know from

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:13.230
what region of the genome each snippet, or
DNA read, came from.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:18.210
And as a result, they have to figure out
how to assemble them together.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.008
To do this, they generate millions of
overlapping reads 

00:03:20.008 --> 00:03:20.835
[INAUDIBLE]

00:03:20.835 --> 00:03:22.340
and use these overlaps 

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.305
To figure out how the reads 

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:26.440
[INAUDIBLE]

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.255
follow each other in the genome. 

00:03:28.255 --> 00:03:28.480
[INAUDIBLE].

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:29.744
Essentially, it is a giant 

00:03:29.744 --> 00:03:31.308
[INAUDIBLE]

00:03:31.308 --> 00:03:32.152
overlap puzzle. 

00:03:32.152 --> 00:03:33.716
[INAUDIBLE].

00:03:33.716 --> 00:03:38.745
Again, what does it have to do with the Seven
Bridges of Konigsberg? 

00:03:38.745 --> 00:03:40.220
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.840
Today, we will learn how to build a
city with

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.680
millions of islands and millions
of bridges in such a way

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:56.265
that every bridge corresponds to a DNA read.

00:03:56.265 --> 00:03:58.415
And after we learn how to walk in

00:03:58.415 --> 00:04:02.400
this megacity, you will be able to
assemble genomes.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.770
I'll meet you at the next bridge, after
we are done.

